# Transient by design: written by self-play and the ladder sweep, then drained and deleted by the
# next elorank run. Never meaningful to share, and a committed copy would be re-merged as duplicate
# games on whatever machine pulled it.
elo-inbox.jsonl
# Scratch, one per running trainer: the model pool handed to self-play workers as a file when the
# list is too long for a command-line argument. Deleted on exit; never meaningful to share.
.selfplay-pool-*.txt
# The official Elo writer's lock. It names a PID and is reclaimed by liveness check, so a copy
# pulled from another machine is at best noise and at worst a foreign PID that happens to be alive
# here, blocking this machine's own writer.
.elo-writer.lock
# The rating store itself: this machine's own league, deliberately NOT shared. Every machine rates
# the models it has against the field it has, and a pulled copy would overwrite that with another
# machine's results -- the independent re-measurement that catches an over-claimed medal is exactly
# what makes a second trainer worth running. Only the derived elo-summary.json is published.
elo-results.json
# One per retromine job: the worker snapshots the script so a mid-run pull cannot change it
# underneath itself. Deleted when the job ends.
.retromine-worker-*.js
# Per-worker self-play shards, merged into their batch file and unlinked immediately after.
data/*.jsonl.w[0-9]*
# The pre-machine-naming shared copy. Superseded by zpd-pool-<machine>.json; ignored so the stale
# local file cannot be re-added and start the same conflict over again.
zpd-pool.json
